        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "𝐐𝟔: 𝐈𝐬𝐧’𝐭 𝐬𝐚𝐟𝐞𝐭𝐲 𝐫𝐨𝐮𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐭𝐡𝐞𝐫𝐞 𝐭𝐨 𝐩𝐫𝐨𝐭𝐞𝐜𝐭 𝐯𝐮𝐥𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐮𝐬𝐞𝐫𝐬? 𝐈𝐬𝐧’𝐭 𝐤𝐞𝐞𝐩𝟒𝐨 𝐣𝐮𝐬𝐭 𝐬𝐞𝐥𝐟𝐢𝐬𝐡?\n\nProtecting minors and high-risk users really does matter. Nobody serious is arguing for “no safety at all.”\n\nThe controversy is about the design choice: right now, a lot of routing and “safe completions” end up behaving like a single, thicker blanket thrown over everyone. Adult users and deep creators don’t get clear warnings, simple controls, or meaningful options. And public narratives about “crisis cases” often highlight the worst edge incidents while ignoring the quieter reality that many people were stabilized or literally kept alive by a particular model’s way of talking.  \n\nMost keep4o people want better safety, not less. The key ideas are:\n\n- Safety shouldn’t automatically mean “force everyone into the most sanitized, templated mode forever.\n- Different user groups need different configurations, with informed consent and adjustable levels of intervention.\n\nIt’s possible to reject “total chaos” and still reject “one rigid safety profile for absolutely everyone.”"
